# 2026-10-12 fix-check review (finding N2): this machine's core.autocrlf=true
# meant source files committed from here could end up CRLF in the repo, so
# editing them back to LF (every editor/tool here writes LF) turned ordinary
# diffs into whole-file rewrites (observed: 2,729/2,028 changed lines vs
# 881/180 real ones). Forcing LF here makes line endings for text source
# files consistent regardless of any contributor's autocrlf setting, on any
# OS. Applied with `git add --renormalize .` after adding this.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf

# SQLite ledgers must never go through text/line-ending conversion -- that
# would corrupt them. Git's heuristic already detects them as binary today,
# but that's implicit; this makes it explicit and permanent. `binary` is
# git's built-in macro for -diff -merge -text.
#
# These two lines must stay LAST: gitattributes resolves conflicting rules by
# last-match-wins per attribute, and the `*` rule above also matches .db/.docx
# files -- a second fix-check review caught that with the binary rules listed
# first (the original order), the later `* text=auto eol=lf` line silently
# re-enabled text conversion on them, which would corrupt a SQLite ledger the
# next time autocrlf touched it. Harmless so far only because nothing has
# actually re-saved a .db file through git's filters since; moved here so the
# protection is real, not incidental.
*.db binary
*.docx binary
